WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.272
Every address a program uses is virtual.

2
00:00:05.272 --> 00:00:10.159
On every load and store, the hardware turns it into a place in physical RAM.

3
00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:12.668
Memory comes in pages of 4 KB.

4
00:00:12.668 --> 00:00:16.273
The low 12 bits are the offset inside the page.

5
00:00:16.273 --> 00:00:18.728
The other 36 bits name the page.

6
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:22.868
A flat table for all those pages would take 512 GB.

7
00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:28.653
So the table is a tree, and the page number splits into four 9-bit indices.

8
00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:31.629
The walk starts at the root table.

9
00:00:31.629 --> 00:00:34.555
Entry 511 points to a level 1 table.

10
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:44.395
Entry 509 there points to level 2, entry 18 to level 3, and entry 1 holds the frame number: where the page sits in RAM.

11
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.423
Add the offset, and you have the physical byte.

12
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:51.245
Four extra reads on every access would be slow.

13
00:00:51.245 --> 00:00:56.009
So the CPU caches translations in the TLB: page in, frame out.

14
00:00:56.233 --> 00:01:00.357
The next access to this page hits the TLB and skips the walk.

15
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:03.664
Now the program asks mmap for one gigabyte.

16
00:01:03.664 --> 00:01:08.274
The kernel only writes down a region: these addresses are valid.

17
00:01:08.274 --> 00:01:09.931
No entries, no RAM yet.

18
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:13.144
The program writes its first byte there.

19
00:01:13.144 --> 00:01:16.907
The TLB misses, and the walk finds entry 66 empty.

20
00:01:16.907 --> 00:01:20.820
The CPU raises a page fault: a jump into the kernel.

21
00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:26.096
The kernel finds the address inside the new region, so this is a valid first touch.

22
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:33.475
It takes a free frame, fills it with zeros, builds the missing level 3 table, and writes the frame's number in.

23
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.767
The CPU runs the store again.

24
00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:40.328
Now the walk finds the frame, and the TLB keeps the translation.

25
00:01:40.328 --> 00:01:42.893
That fault took about a microsecond.

26
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.878
Touching the whole gigabyte takes 262,144 faults like this.

27
00:01:47.878 --> 00:01:54.761
That's demand paging: the kernel hands out memory one page at a time, on first touch.
